title: A child removed from a subscriber hides the subscribee's member it overrides. Since 2026-11-14.
pid: 27c41a2f-aa93-4db5-8990-d05d5b429e7a
body: |2-
  Every contains list records the original; an overrider is drawn in
  its place only where its author's overrides are followed
  ('docs/sharing-model.org', "It round-trips to the original on save").
  So when an author forks a member of his own
  note's content, as Jeff does to answer under a note of Top's, his
  note still names the original. Top placed Jeff's fork in his own fork
  of that note and removed it, the gesture that dismisses any other
  child, and no hide was recorded: the removed ID was in no
  subscribee's contains, and hides were inferred by ID alone.
  Now a removed child that is no member of a subscribee's content, but
  overrides one, hides that member, unless the same save leaves the
  subscriber containing it ('member_it_stands_for' in
  'server/from_text/local_fieldintent_collection/resolve_visibility.rs').
  The walk follows anyone's override records the skgrepo restriction
  shows, not only one owner's: which member a child stands for is a
  fact about the child, whichever perspective the user found it in.
  Top met Jeff's reply in Jeff's perspective; his own fork's view draws
  the same member raw, as his original. A reviewer argued for following
  only the perspective's overrides; I kept anyone's, so the gesture
  works from wherever the reply was read.
  Placing an overrider drops no hide of its original. I built that
  too, for symmetry, and took it out: containing the overrider does
  not integrate the original (the child below), so the original came
  back as unintegrated. The hide is recorded
  against the original, because that is what the unintegrated subtraction
  compares and where the privacy of the explaining record is read; so
  the '1H' herald shows on the original, not on the fork.
contains:
- 1177bfc8-64ef-44c1-a6f9-396ab425a990
